\id 3JN - Restored Context Bible (RCB)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h 3 John
\toc1 The Third Letter of John
\toc2 3 John
\toc3 3Jn
\mt1 3 John

\imt Introduction
\ip The writer names himself only as "the elder," exactly as he does in 2 John, and offers no other credential. His vocabulary is the vocabulary of 1 John and the Gospel of John — truth, love, walking, testimony, being "of God" — and this letter belongs with those: same hand, same period, somewhere in the Greek-speaking world. He has oversight of a group of assemblies rather than one, and the strain in that arrangement is what the letter is about. At fifteen verses it is the shortest document in the New Testament.
\ip It is also the most ordinary-looking letter in the collection: a named sender, a named recipient, an opening wish for the reader's health, a body of specific business, a plan to visit, closing greetings. It goes to one man, Gaius — a very common name in the Roman world, and there is nothing here to connect him with any of the other men so called in Acts or in Paul's letters.
\ip The business is hospitality. The elder sends travelling workers out to the assemblies in his network. They take no money from outsiders, so wherever they arrive somebody has to house them, feed them, and pay for the next stage of the road. Gaius has done this, and word has come back. But in another assembly a man named Diotrephes has refused the elder's men, refused the elder himself, talked against him, and expelled members who wanted to take the travellers in. The elder cannot reach that assembly. So he writes to a man he can reach.
\ip Three named men carry the argument: Gaius, who does the good; Diotrephes, who does the evil; and Demetrius, commended at the close and most likely the man carrying this letter to Gaius's door. Between them stands the letter's one sentence of theology (verse 11), which turns a quarrel over hospitality into a question of who belongs to God. 2 John should be read beside it — there the elder tells an assembly whom not to take in, here he tells a man whom to take in, and they are the two halves of one problem.

\c 1
\s1 The Elder Greets Gaius and Rejoices at the Reports of Him
\p
\v 1 The elder\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Greek \tl ho presbyteros\tl*, with the article: not "an elder" but the elder, the man they know by that name and need no other for. He introduces himself the same way in 2 John, and with no credentials attached — no apostle, no servant of Christ, nothing. The word could name a man holding office in one congregation, or simply a man senior enough that his standing needed no explaining; what this letter shows is a man sending workers out across several assemblies and expecting to be heard in all of them.\f*, to Gaius\f + \fr 1:1 \ft One of the commonest names in the Roman world. Three other men called Gaius appear elsewhere in the New Testament (Acts 20:4; Romans 16:23; 1 Corinthians 1:14), and nothing here connects him to any of them.\f* the beloved, whom I love in truth.
\v 2 Beloved, I pray that in all things you may go well and be in health, just as it goes well with your soul\f + \fr 1:2 \ft Letters of the period open with a wish of exactly this kind — "before all else I pray that you are well" — and he keeps the convention. What is not conventional is the measure he attaches to it. He has had reports of how this man lives (verse 3), and prays that Gaius's circumstances would come up to the level his inner life has already reached. "Soul" is Greek \tl psychē\tl*, the living man himself — not a part of him that detaches from the rest.\f*.
\v 3 For I rejoiced greatly when brothers came \add [i.e. when the men I send out came back through and reported]\add* and testified to your truth \add [i.e. that the truth has taken hold in you and shows]\add*, just as you walk\f + \fr 1:3 \ft "Walk" is the Hebrew way of naming the whole conduct of a life — \tl halakh\tl*, the road a man goes and the manner of his going. Not what he thinks about the truth but what he does with it, every day, where people can see.\f* in truth.
\v 4 I have no greater joy than this: to hear that my children \add [i.e. those I brought into this faith and still answer for]\add* are walking in the truth.
\v 5 Beloved, you do a faithful thing in whatever you work for the brothers — and these strangers at that \add [i.e. men you had never laid eyes on until they were at your door]\add*\f + \fr 1:5 \ft Travel meant depending on somebody. Inns were costly and disreputable, and a man arriving in a strange town with no host had nowhere to go. Taking in a stranger on nothing but his claim to be a brother cost real money and carried real risk, and the assemblies of this period were working out how to tell a genuine traveller from a fraud (compare Didache 11-13).\f* —
\v 6 who testified to your love before the assembly \add [i.e. they stood up in the gathering here and told us what you had done]\add*. You will do well to send them on their way\f + \fr 1:6 \ft Greek \tl propempō\tl*, a settled term for outfitting a traveller for the next leg — provisions, money, company on the road (Romans 15:24; 1 Corinthians 16:6; Titus 3:13). Not seeing a guest to the door but paying his way onward. And the measure of it is God himself: they are out on his errand, so what they are sent off with should look like him.\f* in a manner worthy of God.
\v 7 For they went out for the sake of the Name\f + \fr 1:7 \ft Simply "the Name," with nothing added. There was only one it could mean, and the reverence is in leaving it unsaid — as when the apostles rejoice at being counted worthy to be dishonored "for the Name" (Acts 5:41).\f*, taking nothing from the nations\f + \fr 1:7 \ft Greek \tl ethnikoi\tl*, those outside the faith. Wandering teachers and philosophers were a familiar sight in that world and a suspect one, and a man paid by his hearers could be bought by them. Refusing outside money keeps the message clean — and leaves the entire cost of the mission with believers, which is exactly the conclusion he draws next.\f*.
\v 8 We, then, ought to support such men, so that we may become fellow workers with the truth \add [i.e. the man who pays for the journey is doing the same work as the man who makes it]\add*.
\v 9 I wrote something to the assembly\f + \fr 1:9 \ft An earlier letter, now lost, sent to the congregation Diotrephes controls. He does not say what was in it, and there is no way to identify it with 1 or 2 John. What matters to him is not what he wrote but what became of it.\f*, but Diotrephes, who loves to be first\f + \fr 1:9 \ft Greek \tl philoprōteuōn\tl*, a word found nowhere else and apparently made for the man. It is built on \tl prōteuō\tl*, "to hold first place" — the verb used of the Christ, that in everything he might be first (Colossians 1:18). The charge is not that Diotrephes holds authority but that he loves holding it. Note also what he is not accused of: unlike the men of 2 John, nothing is said against what he believes. The quarrel is over who runs the assembly.\f* among them, does not receive us \add [i.e. he will not have me, nor anyone I send]\add*.
\v 10 For this reason, if I come, I will bring up the works he is doing \add [i.e. I will set them out where the whole assembly can hear them]\add*, babbling against us with evil words\f + \fr 1:10 \ft Greek \tl phlyareō\tl*, to talk empty malicious nonsense; it occurs nowhere else in the New Testament. The words are both worthless and vicious — "evil" here is \tl ponēros\tl*, the word this writer uses of the evil one himself (1 John 5:19).\f*. And not satisfied with that, he does not receive the brothers himself, and those who want to he prevents, and he throws them out of the assembly\f + \fr 1:10 \ft Greek \tl ekballō\tl*, the word used of driving the traders out of the temple and of the healed blind man put out of the synagogue (John 2:15; 9:34). Not a quiet cooling of relations but formal expulsion — and what his own people are expelled for is wanting to feed a traveller.\f*.
\v 11 Beloved, do not imitate the evil but the good \add [i.e. you have Diotrephes in front of you now; do not take him for your pattern]\add*. The one who does good is of God; the one who does evil has not seen God\f + \fr 1:11 \ft "Of God" is this writer's standing test of where a man came from: conduct shows origin, and nothing else does (1 John 3:10; 4:7). And "seen" is not eyesight — it is the encounter that changes how a man lives, so that a life left unchanged is proof there was none (1 John 3:6). Set against verses 9-10 the verdict is plain, and severe: the man who has taken first place in that assembly has never met God.\f*.
\v 12 Demetrius has been testified to by all, and by the truth itself \add [i.e. his life and the message agree, and that agreement is a witness in its own right]\add*. And we testify as well, and you know that our testimony is true\f + \fr 1:12 \ft A letter of recommendation inside a letter. Such letters were the ordinary way of introducing a stranger to people with no means of checking him (compare 2 Corinthians 3:1), and Demetrius is most likely the man carrying this one — arriving at Gaius's door with the elder's vouching for him in hand, and so the first test of what verses 5-8 have just asked. Three witnesses are named, the number a charge or a commendation was required to have (Deuteronomy 19:15).\f*.
\v 13 I had many things to write to you, but I do not want to write to you with ink and reed\f + \fr 1:13 \ft The writing kit of the day: lampblack ink and a sharpened reed. He ends 2 John almost word for word the same way, and means the same thing — what is left to say is too much, and too personal, for a letter.\f*.
\v 14 I hope to see you soon, and we will speak mouth to mouth\f + \fr 1:14 \ft The phrase is literal, and it is the one the Greek scriptures use of God speaking with Moses: "Mouth to mouth I speak to him, and in plain sight, and not in riddles" (Numbers 12:8). Speech with nothing standing in between.\f*.
\v 15 Peace\f + \fr 1:15 \ft Greek \tl eirēnē\tl* renders Hebrew \tl shalom\tl* — not quiet, and not the absence of trouble, but wholeness, everything standing as it should. It is the risen Lord's greeting in John 20:19.\f* to you. The friends\f + \fr 1:15 \ft Greek \tl hoi philoi\tl*. No other letter in the New Testament uses it as a name for the community; they are "brothers," or "the holy ones." It is most likely taken from the Lord's own words: "No longer do I call you slaves... I have called you friends" (John 15:15).\f* greet you. Greet the friends by name \add [i.e. each of them, one at a time, and not as a crowd]\add*.
